Consider a large forested landscape partitioned into harvest units (cut-blocks) and connected by a potential road network represented as a graph whose arcs correspond to possible road segments (each carrying a fixed construction cost and a variable transportation cost). Over a finite tactical planning horizon divided into multiple periods, decisions to be made include: (i) which cut-blocks to harvest in each period, (ii) which road segments to build, upgrade, or maintain in each period, and (iii) how to route harvested timber (possibly of several assortments) from cut-blocks through the road network to demand points (mills, terminals).

The model must incorporate:
- seasonality of road access and harvest operations (period-dependent capacities and costs);
- flow conservation and capacity restrictions on the road network, including the fixed-charge structure that a road must be built before timber can be routed over it;
- demand satisfaction at destinations for each timber assortment in each period;
- spatial constraints such as maximum opening size / adjacency restrictions on simultaneously harvested cut-blocks, green-up delays, and contiguous old-growth or wildlife retention patches;
- environmental constraints such as restrictions on road density in sensitive areas, buffers around streams, and habitat connectivity.

Formulate a mixed-integer programming model that simultaneously optimizes harvesting, transportation, and road-building decisions under the above spatial and environmental constraints, and devise an exact or approximate solution method that can solve instances at the scale of real large industrial forests (on the order of tens of thousands of cut-blocks and road segments) within practical computation times. Specifically: provide a formulation together with valid inequalities, decomposition schemes, or metaheuristic algorithms that yield provably good (or empirically high-quality) integrated tactical plans at this scale.

